          "headline": "Traffic-analysis benchmark fingerprints the model behind an encrypted cloud LLM session with 97.7% balanced accuracy",
          "sources": [
            { "name": "arXiv", "url": "https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.31877" }
          ],
          "bullets": [
            "arXiv:2609.31877, \"A Large-Scale Benchmark and Risk Assessment of Traffic Analysis Attacks on Cloud LLM Services\" (Chalmers University of Technology, University of Texas at El Paso, Old Dominion University, George Mason University), builds a benchmark of \"60,000 user--LLM interactions across 10 models and 6 prompt categories, plus 2,838 multi-agent executions covering 10 task categories and two coordination topologies\".",
            "Using only encrypted packet metadata, the paper reports \"Model fingerprinting achieves 97.7% balanced accuracy, prompt-category fingerprinting reaches 76.7% mean accuracy, and multi-agent task fingerprinting achieves up to 90.7% accuracy\".",
            "The paper reports \"Prompt reformulation weakens but does not remove model-specific leakage, and task fingerprints remain detectable even from a single agent's traffic\" — meaning a network observer can infer which model an organisation uses and roughly what for, without breaking TLS. It is a preprint and has not been independently reproduced."
          ],
          "topics": ["agent-security", "privacy", "surveillance"],
          "impact": "harmful",
          "flags": ["preprint", "single-source"]
        }
