      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Anthropic shipped a model on the same day. Claude Sonnet 5.5." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Anthropic says it runs 30% or more faster and costs up to 30% less for most work than Sonnet 5. On its own benchmark table, Terminal-Bench 4.0 goes from 10.3% to 70.6%, and CursorBench 4.0 from 34.1% to 55.5%." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What about price?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Unchanged. $2 per million input tokens, $10 per million output tokens. It's on Amazon Web Services, Google Cloud and Microsoft Azure." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And every one of those figures is Anthropic's own — a company claim, none of it independently reproduced." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "One line in the announcement is worth flagging. Anthropic says this is the first Sonnet model to launch with the cyber safeguards it uses for its most capable models, because its cybersecurity capabilities are comparable to Opus 5's." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "transition",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "Which brings us to the research, where the question of how fast this is all moving got a formal answer." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "22 researchers including Pachocki, Clark, Horvitz, Hinton and Bengio ask governments for visibility into automated AI R&D",
      "lines": [
        { "host": "B", "text": "The Cambridge Programme on AI Science and Policy published a working paper titled, what if automating AI research and development triggers an intelligence explosion." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And the authors are not outsiders." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "No. Jakub Pachocki of OpenAI, Jack Clark of Anthropic, Eric Horvitz of Microsoft, Geoffrey Hinton, Yoshua Bengio, Andrew Barto and Dawn Song, among 22 in total." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "What's the headline figure?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The paper reports that Anthropic's share of approved code written by AI rose from low single digits to over 80% between January 2025 and May 2026, and that the proportion of research work finished autonomously, with only high-level human supervision, rose from 1% to 26% between March and August 2026." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "And what are they asking for?" },
        { "host": "B", "text": "Limits on how far capabilities can rise in a given period. Independent auditors embedded inside AI companies. Oversight of the data centres running automated research, including options to pause specific workloads. And air-gapped networks for some evaluations." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "Two caveats. It's a working paper — a preprint, not peer reviewed. And those automation percentages are figures the labs report about themselves, so they're company claims that nobody outside has audited." }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "item",
      "section": "Research & papers",
      "headline": "Instruction tuning multiplies high-confidence factual errors by 10x to 35x against base models",
