      "lines": [
        { "host": "A", "text": "There's a second paper worth your time, from the Chinese Academy of Sciences." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "What does it find?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That across five model families, unaligned base models produce few high-confidence errors on long-tail factual questions, while instruction-tuned models multiply those high-confidence errors by 10x to 35x." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "So the step that makes a model useful is also the step that makes its wrong answers sound certain." },
        { "host": "A", "text": "That's the claim. They trace it with Logit Lens probing to the late layers, where wrong-answer margins expand past 4.0 points after sitting near zero earlier. And they propose a fix that cuts high-confidence errors by up to 35.3% against standard DPO." },
        { "host": "B", "text": "The limits?" },
        { "host": "A", "text": "It's a preprint, not peer reviewed, it's the only source we have on it, and the mitigation was tested on one 7B model. The paper doesn't say whether it carries over to larger or closed models." }
